# Danger Warning!
Tamil title: அபாய அறிவிப்பு!

> It pecks away, the Korea-fowl,
> It jabs away, the Korea-fowl;
> Tearing Korea into portions,
> It pecks away, the Korea-fowl.
> Upon its crest it shakes and waves
> A little crimson flag;
> Wherever it sees fit, it lays an egg
> And stirs rebellion—the Korea-fowl.

Do not wake up! Kothamangalam Subbu has composed a “poem” of this kind, and *Ananda Vikatan* has published it under the title “They Are Sounding the Depth.”

The enthusiasm that the Korean question, which is shaking the world, inspired in this “poet of the new age” has crowed in the shape of a fowl—like this.

Do not ask, “A *koli*? What mockery is this?” Through the poet's astonishing imaginative power, *kozhi*, a chicken, has become *koli*, a Korea-fowl. This person who “develops Tamil” has discerned the subtle truth that the Korean question is a sharp spear thrust at world peace. In emotional ecstasy and with the grand wisdom that information must be donated to the people even if sweet classical Tamil is tortured, he has transformed the chicken into a Korea-fowl.

Not merely Subbu, but even the ordinary Appu knows how cruel the Korean question has become today.

For exactly fifteen days as of today, 10 July 1950, American imperialism has used South Korea as its chessboard and prepared for a Third World War.

The United Nations, founded for world peace, sings accompaniment to this American course. Concealing capitalist imperial frenzy behind the curtain of the United Nations, America draws up plans intensely and brings troops to accumulate in Asia.

The place of war is Korea. Those fighting are two sections of the same country—kinsmen quarrelling while each accuses the other: “Your course is wrong.” Why must America display such haste, passion and agitation in this affair? America roams about claiming to labour for world peace. Why send its armed forces instead of resolving the dispute and finding a settlement?

American aircraft and troops have been sent to fight for South Korea.

On the seventh of this month President Truman proclaimed compulsory military service in America.

America is working with such intensity to turn the Korean War into a Third World War. It is dragging Russia towards war. Throwing dust into the eyes of the world's people, capitalism girds itself to oppose communism.

Every country ruled under capitalism supports this course.

Because the institutions that supply news to the whole world are in the hands of American capitalists, they popularise the message that Russia and North Korea alone caused the Korean War.

Yet although so many days have passed since the war began, Russia has issued not even a small statement about its connection with the conflict. Nor have these agencies reported Russian forces taking part.

America beats the war drum, while Russia remains silent as though mocking it.

Still, the artillery of capitalist propaganda fires upon Russia. India's nationalist periodicals also write attacks upon Russia. Following that line, *Ananda Vikatan* has published the “Korea-fowl,” shaking a red flag upon its crest and pecking at Korea. Capitalist countries do not hesitate to accuse Russia; the Indian government and people allied with them follow the same “style.”

People such as ourselves have from time to time criticised the methods Russia uses to spread its doctrine of common ownership.

It turns Communists in every country into people who “look towards Moscow,” training them to act according to its orders.

Communism is one thing; communist imperialism is another. The noble doctrine that the heart of the working person should become a furnace and that paths must be found for that person's advancement is today turning into an imperial road.

Russia seeks to establish an empire through communism. Crying “Communism everywhere,” it creates people who look towards Moscow. With fanatical haste, it allows violence to raise its head in many countries.

Many scholars who possess faith and interest in the doctrine of common ownership have frequently raised voices of condemnation against this Russian course.

They say that, under the pretext that communism must spread, Russia desires to become the centre of communist imperialism.

We do not wish either to deny or conceal this assertion.

Russia deliberately works to turn countries into dancers who move at its command, using communism as its justification. Our question is not whether this course is necessary. It is fully true that Russia makes efforts just as intensely as American capitalism labours to establish its own domination.

As a result, every communist country remains within Russia's tent, with the sole exception of Yugoslavia.

Through Communist parties operating in various countries, Russia creates a desire to realise the ideal dream by violence and outrage.

Indian Communists too have fallen into this difficulty without considering conditions here. By walking the destructive path of violence and conspiracy, they have earned immense public hostility.

America attempts to turn the fear and alarm produced among the people by Communist violence into an opportunity to gather overwhelming support for its own camp.

Countries caught in America's enchanting net likewise pretend merely to condemn Communist violence while trying to create hostility among people towards communism itself.

Communists possess a significant presence in many Asian countries. Indo-China, Indonesia, Burma, Malaya, India, Pakistan, Ceylon and other countries acquired a new status after the last war.

India, Indonesia, Burma, Pakistan and Ceylon in particular freed themselves from the grip of European imperialism and became independent countries.

In newly independent states, power and authority passed into the hands of parties that had fought for self-rule.

Seeing power pass to the heroes who had beaten the drum of liberation and achieved victory, capitalists in each country tried to make those parties their friends.

As soon as India became independent and established a government of self-rule, many Indian capitalists became Congressmen.

Under British rule, wealthy men had lived merrily in coats and suits, with the Union Jack flying upon the flagstaff and a British-bestowed “Sir” before their names. They now assumed a new appearance.

Coats and suits disappeared and khadi was donned. A Congress flag was tied where the Union Jack had flown.

Capitalism's way is to put on the costume appropriate to the occasion. The wealthy therefore adopted the proper new guise.

They praised Nehru and paid homage to Patel as never before, demonstrating their “immeasurable love of country.” They put on the new costume, and success came swiftly.

Congress leaders became puppets of the wealthy. Indian capitalism grew into a powerful force that could make the country dance. Within a short time after Congress ascended the seat of government, about twenty-six capitalists became India's uncrowned kings, wire-pullers hidden behind the scenes who made the government dance.

Because the Congress party had concentrated chiefly upon the freedom struggle, it possessed no sound programme for the country's economic and social advancement. Nor was it in a condition to work intensely. Once independence arrived, it became like a boat caught in a storm, satisfied if the machinery of government could somehow continue to run.

It had to cope with the difficulties of newly assuming great responsibility, the grave post-war conditions in the country and, above all, the economic crisis.

Lacking the courage to overcome these troubles, it struggled for money to carry out the programmes the country required.

Indian capitalism quickly turned these circumstances to its advantage. American capitalist rule began acting through sweet words mixed with poison until India called it an “intimate friend.”

America rejoiced that it had found a good companion for destroying the growing movement for common ownership.

When those who came to rule had fought for independence, they had roared heroically that they would accomplish many things. The people therefore expected many benefits to blossom. They received only disappointment.

Fearing that this would surround them with a bad name and harm their party, the rulers undertook the sacred task of magnifying minor issues and turning public attention towards them.

“What can we do? Now that the country has been divided, the refugee problem has grown,” they said.

“Alas! Attend to the Kashmir dispute. See Hyderabad's outrages,” they cried.

“Can the conduct of these Communists be tolerated? They do what must not be done!” they lamented.

Though they created many questions to suit each occasion, they never hesitated to sing the same refrain again and again: “Destructive forces have grown in the country. We must toil terribly to suppress the Communists and save people from the path of violence. What can we do?”

Indian Communists who followed “the path shown by Moscow” made themselves eligible for this accusation.

America observed the Indian government trying to escape public anger by displaying the Communist bogey, and Indian capitalism making that government dance through cooperation. It celebrated Nehru as its friend, pretended to show immense concern for India and stamped India as a member of its capitalist camp.

Nehru and others have proclaimed, and continue to proclaim: “India will join neither the capitalist camp nor the communist tent. It will follow an independent policy of neutrality and labour for world peace.”

We have said that this is impossible—wisdom preaching compromise after placing one's head inside a tiger's mouth. We have warned that the Nehru government is becoming a servant of dollar imperialism.

Those who refused to believe us then now have an opportunity to see clearly.

The United Nations adopted America's resolution calling for military assistance to South Korea.

When the resolution was introduced and votes taken in the Security Council, India did not participate.

News reports said many Americans were distressed to learn that India had not voted. Francis Biddle, leader of the American liberals, announced his disappointment. He issued a lengthy statement saying he had expected India to join America in taking action against North Korea and regretted that it had abstained.

Many suspicions arose over India's failure to vote upon the resolution concerning the North Korean war.

“There is no reason! A minor difficulty arose in telegraphic communication between America and India. The Indian government therefore could not send instructions to its representative, Sir B. N. Rau,” Nehru's department announced.

But the very next day India proclaimed that it accepted the United Nations resolution.

An important meeting occurred in New Delhi on the preceding day, 29 June, before that proclamation was issued.

Upon hearing that India had not participated in the vote, Loy Henderson, the American ambassador in India, reportedly went to Nehru's residence and spoke with him at length.

America shows extraordinary concern over the Korean War. India, a major Asian country, did not participate in the vote. The American ambassador immediately met the Indian Prime Minister. The next day India proclaimed its support for the American resolution.

Study these events closely and the subtlety becomes clear. One can infer what the American ambassador may have said to Nehru.

“They boast vainly and fall!”

“They surrender at the feet of the enchantress of money; the result will be disastrous!”

“The Nehru government regards America as an intimate friend—but America is a smiling spider.”

“They take loan after loan from America. This mortgages India to America and drops it into the capitalist net.”

“This course is not good. It is not good for the heroes who came to govern the country to behave this way.”

We have repeatedly given such warnings. We argued that India must possess a stable foreign policy and must not, through vanity and haste, undertake disastrous actions. They erupted in anger: “What business is it of these people? They envy the welcome America gives our Nehru. That is why they babble.”

Indian Prime Minister Nehru spoke as though answering accusations such as ours: “We shall become no one's slaves. Democracy and world prosperity are our aims. We shall neither join America's side nor become Russia's friend. We are not such fools that we would lose the independence we have newly established.”

The nationalist papers used this material to pour out editorials.

But what now? India has accepted the American resolution despite knowing that America's course is preparation for the next war. It has signed the resolution providing military assistance to South Korea.

In New Delhi on 7 July, Nehru declared that because of the Korean War there was a fifty-percent chance of a world war breaking out. Yet he adopted resolutions supporting America, supposedly to protect the honour of the United Nations.

Ceylon, a small country close by, announced that it had no intention of involving itself in the Korean question.

Egypt declared categorically that the Security Council was doing America's work, that justice and honesty were absent, and that the United Nations had become a hall of American autocracy. It therefore refused to accept the UN resolution.

*[The source repeats the preceding warnings and the Ceylon and Egypt passages across Images 10 and 11.]*

The Nehru government, however, remained silent for one day and then proclaimed its support for the resolution to provide military assistance to South Korea.

“Why support the UN resolution to help South Korea?”

“Because one country must not invade another.”

“What action did America take before the resolution was passed by the United Nations?”

“It sent military, naval and air forces. The American navy blockaded the China Sea around Formosa. It appointed General MacArthur to conduct the Korean War.”

“That means America entered the war directly.”

“That is how it must be understood.”

“Would that not cause the next war to break out?”

“It might. There is a fifty-percent chance of a world war.”

“Very well. Why is America so eager to show such haste and favour on South Korea's behalf?”

“The United Nations principle that one country must not invade another—”

“Then why has America shown no concern whatever about the Kashmir question?”

“Why ask me? You would have to ask the Americans to know!”

The questioner was a reporter; the person answering was Nehru. These were the kinds of answers he gave journalists in New Delhi on 7 July.

When asked why America, which sends troops and money in support of Korea, showed no attention or concern in Kashmir, Indian Prime Minister Nehru angrily answered, “You must ask them.”

Nehru is not unaware why America displays such passion over Korea. Yet, having come under a “compulsion” to support the American resolution, he erupts when questioned.

The same question was asked by major Indian newspapers such as *The Times of India*, *Free Press*, *Nation* and *Blitz*. Pandit Nehru's own daily, *National Herald*, raised it too, as though pointing out that the Indian government's decision on Korea was wrong.

The question is entirely reasonable. America had shown no comparable concern in any other issue.

The reason, the editorial argues, is that America seeks to use the Korean dispute to drag Russia forcibly into another war.

Nehru, who administers India's foreign affairs, is not ignorant of that reason. His own declaration that there is a fifty-percent chance of another war makes clear that he understands it.

Despite this, he has drawn India into the Korean question.

Many expected that the Indian government's course after independence would lead precisely to a decision of this kind.

It has no stable policy and changes from minute to minute.

It desires to be America's intimate friend, while also wishing not to become Russia's enemy.

It says it will remain neutral, but its actions support America.

The Nehru government follows its whims in foreign affairs and proceeds without stability.

Many have condemned the Indian government's course in these terms.

If asked whether it showed as much concern in understanding foreign countries' affairs as it showed in sending ambassadors to them, the only answer is doubt.

Regarding Korea, the following list demonstrates the Indian government's instability:

1. India did not vote for the American resolution in the United Nations.
2. The day after the American ambassador met Nehru, India proclaimed support for the American resolution.
3. India said it was prepared to mediate in the Korean question.
4. It then announced that mediation was possible only if both North and South Korea desired it.
5. It stated that the island of Formosa rightfully belonged to China.
6. The American navy entered the waters around Formosa, said to be in aid of Korea.
7. India consented to military assistance for South Korea.
8. It stated that it possessed no troops and could not send any.
9. India did not vote for the UN resolution giving MacArthur full authority to conduct the Korean War.
10. Through Ambassador Dr Radhakrishnan, India gave conciliatory explanations to the Soviet Deputy Foreign Minister about why it had supported the American resolution.

This is only a short list; expand it and it will continue growing. Each item directly contradicts another. Yet all this evolves into their “diplomacy.”

At America's prompting, and from a desire to behave as its intimate friend, they involved India in the Korean question.

Now that condemnation and criticism of their decision have erupted, they publish contradictions as explanations.

They issue one action as though appeasing Russia and another order as though placating America.

It is an amusing spectacle—the result of an unstable mind; the calamity produced by the eclipse of ability; the disaster caused by surrender to the enchantress of capitalism.

There was no need to intervene immediately in Korea or bind India to a resolution requiring military assistance.

Yet through its relationship with America, the Indian government fell prey to this predicament.

Now that the results of its hasty thinking appear as “danger warnings,” it is bewildered and publishes contradictory explanations every day.

America's course is increasingly revealed as preparation for the next world war.

America continues making the necessary arrangements.

At this moment India has an opportunity to establish its distinct character. It must condemn the course that builds a platform for war and gather the support of countries that do not desire war. It must stop America's intervention in Korea, which is marked by extraordinary vested concern, ascertain the wishes of the people living there and act accordingly.

How many times has Nehru roared heroically that his ideal is to drive European imperialism out of Asia?

Will he now perform this substantive work that would benefit the world? Or will he make it his policy to struggle on—first surrendering to the American enchantress and then becoming bewildered when he recognises the danger?

These questions arise not only from our hearts, but from the hearts of everyone who desires India's prosperity.

*Dravida Nadu*, 16 July 1950
